guide='''<p><a href="../START_HERE.html">Back to all packets</a></p><h1>Coordinator guide for the full SchoolOs test</h1><p>Prepare the school and accounts, assign small feature packets, collect the downloaded reports and arrange retests. These documents are a test plan, not evidence that a feature has already passed.</p><div class="notice"><strong>Do not begin on real school records.</strong> Use two disposable schools and controlled inboxes. Keep payments in the provider\'s test mode. Give passwords and activation codes privately, outside the tracker and test reports.</div><h2>What to give each tester</h2><ol><li>The current test app address and their own private sign-in details.</li><li>The entire extracted pack, or their assigned feature HTML file. Each feature file works on its own; the link back to the index needs the full folder.</li><li>The exact test school, role, class, subject, term and case numbers.</li><li>The applicable sample upload files and only the controlled records needed for that task.</li><li>A place to return the downloaded completed report, backup and separately named screenshots.</li></ol><p>A feature packet is usually best split into a small first sitting of 5 to 8 cases. Repeat essential flows with another tester/device. Do not hand every packet to one non-technical person.</p><h2>Before the first tester starts</h2><ul><li>Confirm the staging address and deployment version. The earlier supplied frontend was https://portals-frontend-woad.vercel.app/ and backend was https://portals-backend-kyaf.onrender.com. Testers use the frontend, not the backend address.</li><li>Have the developer apply the current migrations, including student activation, and deploy/restart both apps. Do not ask non-technical testers to run database commands.</li><li>Create School A for the shared journey, School B for privacy checks, and a separate disposable school/class for uploads and destructive experiments.</li><li>Use controlled inboxes for invitations, password resets, Google and bank codes. Confirm email delivery before assigning those cases.</li><li>Confirm whether Google and payment-provider test mode work. Missing configuration means Cannot test, not Passed.</li><li>Prepare every account in account-allocation.csv. Use a unique account per concurrent tester so profile/password/role changes do not conflict.</li><li>Record teaching subjects and class-teacher assignments. A job title alone is not a classroom assignment.</li><li>Prepare the actual phone/desktop browsers. Offline/PWA tests require the production HTTPS app or an approved localhost production build; ordinary development mode is not equivalent.</li><li>Arrange a coordinator to control test backend outages, permission removal and slow-connection tests. Testers should not use technical tools.</li></ul><h2>Prepare the shared records</h2><p>Use sample-files/students-baseline-six.csv after creating JSS 1 A, JSS 2 A and SS 1 A. Create Mathematics and English for normal tests. Give a separate timetable class exactly 13 subjects. Set current term dates that include the attendance test day.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Students</th><th>Link and assignment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>JSS 1 A</td><td>Ada Obi and John Doe</td><td>Both linked to Parent A. Assigned teacher is class teacher and Mathematics teacher.</td></tr><tr><td>JSS 2 A</td><td>Mary Jo and Sarah Joshua</td><td>Mary linked only to Parent B. Use another teacher unless testing multiple class assignments.</td></tr><tr><td>SS 1 A</td><td>Tunde Bello and Maya Okafor</td><td>Senior fee group. Use a separate class teacher where required.</td></tr><tr><td>Without a class</td><td>Nora West in a separate upload test</td><td>Use an unassigned record for that scenario; keep shared fee counts controlled.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Prepare an activated Ada student account, another activated student for privacy checks, and a different unused student for activation-code tests. Parent A needs two children; also prepare one-child and no-child parent accounts. Never overwrite a tester\'s account during another test.</p><h2>Use exact fee figures</h2><p>Run money checks in a clean copy with only the six assigned baseline students and no other active fee rules or payments. Upload/pagination, overpayment, reversal, class-move and new-student tests use separate disposable records or run after the shared baseline is finished.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Junior per student</th><th>Senior per student</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tuition</td><td>NGN 10000</td><td>NGN 12000</td></tr><tr><td>PTA</td><td>NGN 3500</td><td>NGN 3500</td></tr><tr><td>Exam</td><td>NGN 8000</td><td>NGN 9700</td></tr><tr><td>Sports</td><td>NGN 300</td><td>NGN 300</td></tr><tr><td>Library</td><td>NGN 2000</td><td>NGN 2000</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory for SS 1 A only</td><td>NGN 0</td><td>NGN 1000</td></tr><tr><td>Total for each student</td><td>NGN 23800</td><td>NGN 28500</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><strong>Expected total:</strong> four juniors times NGN 23800 plus two seniors times NGN 28500 equals <strong>NGN 152200</strong>. Before payment: received 0; owed 152200.</p><p><strong>After Ada pays 3500:</strong> Ada owes 20300; school received 3500; school owed 148700. <strong>After Ada pays another 20300:</strong> Ada owes 0; school received 23800; school owed 128400.</p><p>Run manual-payment and provider-payment cases on different prepared copies, or reset the baseline deliberately. Do not add the same payment twice because two packets mention it.</p><h2>Choose the assignment by role</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Useful packets</th><th>Boundary to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''
for role,packets,boundary in roles:guide+='<tr><td>'+esc(role)+'</td><td>'+esc(packets)+'</td><td>'+esc(boundary)+'</td></tr>'
guide+='''</tbody></table></div><h2>Suggested run order</h2><ol><li>Confirm deployment, accounts, email and the disposable schools.</li><li>Run 01 to 06 for access, school setup, classes, records and family links.</li><li>Run 07 to 10 for attendance, scores, release and timetables. Prepare 15/15/70 in a separate clean term; changing an in-use marking scheme should be blocked.</li><li>Run 11 then 12 for exact fees and manual payments; run 13 and 14 for parent/student payment flows on separate prepared balances.</li><li>Run 15 and 21 for notices and own account. Schedule destructive/password tests after dependent tasks.</li><li>Run 16 across roles, including a real 48-hour offline follow-up. Prepare/download while online first.</li><li>Run 17 and 18 across roles and physical devices. Run platform packet 19 only with the platform coordinator.</li><li>Finish with packet 20 as a paired school-day rehearsal. Record each failed handoff.</li></ol><h2>Collect reports without losing answers</h2><ul><li>Ask testers for their downloaded report HTML and backup JSON. The optional results CSV is convenient for the tracker.</li><li>Do not collect only the original checklist file; answers are not written into that original file.</li><li>Put returned files in returned-reports, with screenshots named by packet, case and tester. Share them through your chosen private channel; the checklist does not upload them.</li><li>Open a returned report in a browser. It is a readable frozen copy and can be printed/saved as PDF.</li><li>For another tester or role using the same browser and packet, download the previous report/backup first, then open the backup section and choose Start a new tester response. Otherwise use another browser profile/device.</li><li>Use the matching backup to continue editing the same feature packet. Browser saving can be restricted on some devices, so downloads are the reliable handoff.</li><li>Phone browsers differ in support for local HTML. If a phone cannot open it properly, use a computer for the checklist while testing the app on the phone, or print the checklist. Never pretend a blank file contains answers.</li></ul><h2>Review problems and user experience separately</h2><p>Use assignment-tracker.csv for ownership/status and problem-register.csv for issues. One issue may affect several cases; keep the same problem number and record all affected cases.</p><p><strong>Stop and escalate:</strong> another school/family\'s private data, a duplicate charge, wrong payment totals, lost saved work or access granted without permission. <strong>Important:</strong> a main task cannot finish. <strong>Usability:</strong> the task works but needs help, too many clicks or confusing wording. <strong>Appearance:</strong> clipping, weak readability or unattractive layout.</p><p>Ask for four separate ratings: intuitive, easy, beautiful and good overall experience. Yes means it met that description; Partly means only some of it did; No means it did not; Not rated means insufficient use. A working feature can still have poor usability.</p><p><strong>Example problem report:</strong> Case 12-01. As Bursar on Android Chrome, I opened Fees. I expected to find Record payment near the top. I had to scroll through several sections and needed help. Screenshot 12-01-testerA.png. Suggestion: keep the payment action next to the balance.</p><h2>Decide whether the release is ready</h2><p>Record a result for every assigned case. Cannot test and Not tested are outstanding work, not passes. Do not average away a privacy or money failure because appearance scores are good.</p><ul><li>Access, invitations, student activation and role boundaries must pass for the intended users.</li><li>Attendance, score entry, result release and A4 printing must complete without manual database repairs.</li><li>Fees, payments, receipt amounts and retries must reconcile.</li><li>Prepared offline records must remain usable and queued work must sync safely, including after the actual two-day follow-up.</li><li>Parent/student tasks must work on a real phone, and serious usability failures must have an agreed fix or explicit release decision.</li><li>For each remaining problem, record its impact, owner, proposed action and retest date. Obtain the coordinator\'s explicit release decision.</li></ul><h2>Features needing a readiness check</h2><p>Some visible platform screens include local-only/example behaviour. Packet 19 deliberately checks persistence; a success banner alone must not pass. Public-site editing is not currently offered in school Settings, so no tester is asked to find a hidden editor. Legacy PIN screens are not the supported family result route.</p><p>React Native and Electron folders are starter foundations. This pack tests the web app and installed PWA; it does not claim finished native app features. Server-only security, backup, email-worker and payment-verification checks remain a developer responsibility, described in IMPLEMENTATION_COVERAGE.md.</p><h2>Coordinator sign off</h2><p>Run label ____________________  Coordinator ____________________  Date ____________________</p><p>Assigned cases ______  Passed ______  Failed ______  Cannot test ______  Not tested ______</p><p>Decision: [ ] Ready for agreed release  [ ] Retest required  [ ] Do not release</p><p>Outstanding privacy or money problems __________________________________________________</p><p>Approved next steps and owners ________________________________________________________</p><p>Next review date ____________________</p>'''
(OUT/'coordinator/00-coordinator-guide.html').write_text(shell('Coordinator guide',guide,back='../START_HERE.html'),encoding='utf-8')
